1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,815
In this video we'll take Soroban Identity
from a fresh clone to a running app on

2
00:00:08,815 --> 00:00:12,120
Stellar testnet, using SDK version 0.1.0.

3
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:20,384
You'll need Rust, the Stellar CLI, Node 20
or later, and a Stellar wallet like

4
00:00:20,384 --> 00:00:27,546
Freighter. If you'd rather skip the
installs, the repo ships a dev container

5
00:00:27,546 --> 00:00:29,750
with all of it.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,756
Clone the repository and take a look
around.

7
00:00:37,756 --> 00:00:48,421
contracts holds the three Soroban
contracts, sdk is the TypeScript SDK,

8
00:00:48,421 --> 00:01:02,963
frontend is the React app, server is an
optional API server, and scripts has the

9
00:01:02,963 --> 00:01:09,750
deploy script we'll use in a minute.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:22,422
Soroban contracts are written in Rust and
compiled to WebAssembly.

11
00:01:22,422 --> 00:01:39,813
If you don't have Rust, install it with
rustup, then add the wasm32 unknown

12
00:01:39,813 --> 00:01:49,750
unknown target. That's the compilation
target Soroban expects.

13
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:11,931
Next, the Stellar CLI. We use it to deploy
contracts and to call them from the

14
00:02:11,931 --> 00:02:13,302
terminal.

15
00:02:13,302 --> 00:02:29,750
Installing it with cargo takes a few
minutes, so I'll skip ahead.

16
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:39,717
To deploy we need a testnet account with
some test lumens.

17
00:02:39,717 --> 00:02:53,850
The CLI can generate a key and fund it
through Friendbot in one step. I've called

18
00:02:53,850 --> 00:03:08,867
it deployer. The secret stays in the CLI's
key store, so it never has to appear on

19
00:03:08,867 --> 00:03:09,750
screen.

20
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:24,422
The deploy script builds the three
contracts, deploys each one to testnet,

21
00:03:24,422 --> 00:03:34,036
and calls initialize with your address as
admin.

22
00:03:34,036 --> 00:03:55,669
It reads your key from STELLAR SECRET KEY.
The CLI accepts a key name as well as a

23
00:03:55,669 --> 00:04:01,678
secret, so I'm passing deployer.

24
00:04:01,678 --> 00:04:19,705
When it finishes, the contract IDs are
saved to a file called dot env dot

25
00:04:19,705 --> 00:04:35,328
deployed in the repo root. It's
git-ignored, and if you run the script

26
00:04:35,328 --> 00:04:49,750
again it reuses contracts that already
exist instead of deploying new ones.

27
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:13,217
The SDK and the frontend are npm
workspaces, so one install at the root

28
00:05:13,217 --> 00:05:16,533
covers both.

29
00:05:16,533 --> 00:05:39,750
npm run build compiles the SDK first, then
the frontend that depends on it.

30
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:58,385
The frontend reads its contract IDs from
environment variables, with separate sets

31
00:05:58,385 --> 00:06:04,513
for testnet and mainnet.

32
00:06:04,513 --> 00:06:29,026
Copy the example file and paste in the
three IDs from dot env dot deployed, into

33
00:06:29,026 --> 00:06:39,750
the variables that start with VITE
TESTNET.

34
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:48,457
Start the Vite dev server and open the
local URL.

35
00:06:48,457 --> 00:06:58,606
There's the app: Identity, Credentials and
Issuer analytics tabs, a network switcher

36
00:06:58,606 --> 00:07:04,527
and a wallet button in the header.

37
00:07:04,527 --> 00:07:18,059
If a contract ID is missing or wasn't
initialized, a banner at the top tells you

38
00:07:18,059 --> 00:07:19,750
which one.

39
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:34,645
Make sure the app's network switcher says
Testnet, and that your wallet is on

40
00:07:34,645 --> 00:07:47,197
testnet too. If the two disagree, you'll
be looking at different networks.

41
00:07:47,197 --> 00:07:58,704
Click connect, choose Freighter, and
approve. Your address appears in the

42
00:07:58,704 --> 00:07:59,750
header.

43
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:15,478
Let's create our first DID. On the
Identity tab, create a DID for the

44
00:08:15,478 --> 00:08:25,428
connected wallet and approve the
transaction in your wallet.

45
00:08:25,428 --> 00:08:39,800
Then resolve your own address. There's
your DID document: the did colon stellar

46
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:49,750
identifier, the controller, timestamps,
and active set to true.

47
00:08:50,000 --> 00:09:05,143
You can always check the chain directly.
Load the IDs from dot env dot deployed and

48
00:09:05,143 --> 00:09:14,607
call has active did for our address. It
returns true.

49
00:09:14,607 --> 00:09:28,804
Everything the app does is plain contract
calls, so the CLI is a great debugging

50
00:09:28,804 --> 00:09:29,750
tool.

51
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:40,743
That's it: contracts on testnet, the app
running locally, and your first DID.

52
00:09:40,743 --> 00:09:53,139
In the next video we'll look closely at
DIDs: what's in the document, how metadata

53
00:09:53,139 --> 00:09:59,750
works, and how to update and deactivate
one.
